{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-164101",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence, $A$ and $C$ are local minima.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14233384/assets/0262a775dad79697ea888b6047e607c12ec804dbb734b72b2ca8fda54d6c130f.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
